@subsection The Machine Model

The machine model generated by @code{make-machine} is represented as a Rust
struct: its registers, operations, instruction sequence, stack, and monitoring
counters are ordinary fields, and nothing outside the module touches them
directly.  To build this model, @code{make-machine} begins by calling
the procedure @code{make-new-machine} to construct the parts of the machine
model that are common to all register machines.  This basic machine model
constructed by @code{make-new-machine} is essentially a container for some
registers and a stack, together with an execution mechanism that processes the
controller instructions one by one.

@code{make_machine} then extends this basic model to
include the registers, operations, and controller of the particular machine
being defined.  First it allocates a register in the new machine for each of
the supplied register names and installs the designated operations in the
machine.  Then it uses an @newterm{assembler} (described below in 
@ref{5.2.2}) to transform the controller text into instructions for the new
machine and installs these as the machine's instruction sequence.
@code{Make-machine} returns as its value the modified machine model.

@example rust
pub fn make_machine(
    register_names: &[&str],
    operations: &[(&str, OpHandler)],
    controller_text: &str,
) -> Result<Machine, Fault> {
    make_machine_from_datums(
        register_names,
        operations,
        parse_controller(controller_text)?,
    )
}
@end example

@subsubheading Registers

In this edition a register is the machine's table entry for its name: the
table maps each register name to the value the register holds.  The machine's
@code{allocate_registers} creates the registers that can be accessed or
changed:

@example rust
impl Machine {
    /// Allocates one table entry per name, initialized to
    /// the book's `*unassigned*` sentinel; a repeated name
    /// is a typed fault.
    pub fn allocate_registers(&mut self, names: &[&str]) -> Result<(), Fault> {
        for name in names {
            if self.registers.contains_key(*name) {
                return Err(Fault::DuplicateRegister {
                    reg: (*name).to_owned(),
                });
            }
            self.registers
                .insert((*name).to_owned(), Value::sym("*unassigned*"));
        }
        Ok(())
    }
}
@end example

@noindent
The following methods are the machine's whole register interface:

@example rust
impl Machine {
    /// The single read path: every reader goes through
    /// here; a missing register is a typed fault.
    fn read(&self, name: &str) -> Result<Value, Fault> {
        self.registers
            .get(name)
            .cloned()
            .ok_or_else(|| Fault::UnknownRegister {
                reg: name.to_owned(),
            })
    }

    /// The single store path: assigns, restores, and the
    /// traced-register reports of exercise 5.18 all store
    /// through here.
    fn store(&mut self, name: &str, value: Value) -> Result<(), Fault> {
        let old = self.read(name)?;
        if self.traced_registers.contains(name) {
            let line = format!(
                "{}: {} -> {}",
                name,
                display_value(&old),
                display_value(&value)
            );
            self.transcript.push(line);
        }
        self.registers.insert(name.to_owned(), value);
        Ok(())
    }
}
@end example

@subsubheading The stack

In this edition the machine owns the stack: a list of tagged entries, each
pairing the register name that saved a value with the value itself, the record
exercise 5.18 needs.  The machine accepts requests to @code{push} an entry
onto the stack, to @code{pop} the top entry off the stack and return it, and
to @code{initialize} the stack to empty; popping an empty stack is a typed
underflow fault, not a runtime crash.

@example rust
impl Machine {
    /// Pushes one tagged entry onto the stack: the
    /// register name rides beside the value, the record
    /// exercise 5.18 needs.
    fn push(&mut self, reg: &str, value: Value) {
        self.stack.push((reg.to_owned(), value));
    }

    /// Pops the top entry; an empty stack is a typed
    /// underflow fault naming the restoring register.
    fn pop(&mut self, reg: &str) -> Result<(String, Value), Fault> {
        self.stack
            .pop()
            .ok_or(Fault::StackUnderflow {
                reg: reg.to_owned(),
            })
    }
}
@end example

@noindent
The save and restore instructions call these methods and nothing else does;
@code{initialize_stack} empties the stack and resets the counters:

@example rust
impl Machine {
    /// Empties the stack and resets the monitoring
    /// counters added in @ref{5.2.4}.
    fn initialize_stack(&mut self) {
        self.stack.clear();
        self.depth = 0;
        self.pushes = 0;
        self.max_depth = 0;
    }
}
@end example

@subsubheading The basic machine

The @code{make-new-machine} procedure, shown in @ref{Figure 5.13,,@w{Figure 5.13}}, 
constructs
a value whose state consists of a register table, a stack, an initially empty
instruction sequence, a list of operations that initially contains an operation
to initialize the stack, and the monitoring counters of @ref{5.2.4}.  The
register table initially contains one register, named @code{flag}; the book's
second table entry, @code{pc}, is in this edition a program-counter index into
the instruction sequence.  The method @code{allocate_registers} adds new
entries to the register table, and the read path shown above looks up registers
in the table.

@noindent
@strong{@anchor{Figure 5.13}Figure 5.13:} @math{\downarrow} The @code{make-new-machine}
procedure, which implements the basic machine model.

@example rust
fn make_new_machine() -> Machine {
    let mut machine = Machine {
        registers: HashMap::new(),
        ops: HashMap::new(),
        insts: Vec::new(),
        labels: HashMap::new(),
        stack: Vec::new(),
        per_register: BTreeMap::new(),
        discipline: RestoreDiscipline::Plain,
        depth: 0,
        pushes: 0,
        max_depth: 0,
        instruction_count: 0,
        budget: None,
        trace: false,
        trace_labels: false,
        traced_registers: BTreeSet::new(),
        breakpoints: BTreeSet::new(),
        held: None,
        transcript: Vec::new(),
        use_summary: InstructionUse::default(),
        pc: 0,
        flag: Value::sym("*unassigned*"),
    };
    // The flag is an ordinary register in the table: it
    // starts at the book's *unassigned* sentinel, and only
    // a test ever writes it.
    machine
        .registers
        .insert("flag".to_owned(), Value::sym("*unassigned*"));
    machine
}
@end example

The @code{flag} register is used to control branching in the simulated machine.
@code{Test} instructions set the contents of @code{flag} to the result of the
test (true or false).  @code{Branch} instructions decide whether or not to
branch by examining the contents of @code{flag}.

The program counter determines the sequencing of instructions as the machine
runs.  This sequencing is implemented by the @code{execute} method.
In the simulation model, each machine instruction is a data structure that
includes a procedure of no arguments, called the @newterm{instruction execution procedure}, 
such that calling this procedure simulates executing the
instruction.  As the simulation runs, @code{pc} points to the place in the
instruction sequence beginning with the next instruction to be executed.
@code{Execute} gets that instruction, executes it by calling the instruction
execution procedure, and repeats this cycle until there are no more
instructions to execute (i.e., until @code{pc} points to the end of the
instruction sequence).

@noindent
As part of its operation, each instruction execution procedure modifies
@code{pc} to indicate the next instruction to be executed.  @code{Branch} and
@code{goto} instructions change @code{pc} to point to the new destination.  All
other instructions simply advance @code{pc}, making it point to the next
instruction in the sequence.  Observe that each call to @code{execute} calls
@code{execute} again, but this does not produce an infinite loop because
running the instruction execution procedure changes the contents of @code{pc}.

Notice that starting the machine is
accomplished by setting the program counter to the beginning of the instruction
sequence and running @code{execute}:

For convenience, we provide an alternate procedural interface to a machine's
@code{start} operation, as well as procedures to set and examine register
contents, as specified at the beginning of @ref{5.2}:

@example rust
impl Machine {
    /// The driver loop: one instruction execution
    /// procedure per turn, counting every step, until the
    /// sequence runs out or a fault stops the run.
    fn execute(&mut self) -> Result<Stop, Fault> {
        loop {
            if self.pc >= self.insts.len() {
                return Ok(Stop::Done);
            }
            self.trace_step();
            self.instruction_count += 1;
            let exec = Rc::clone(&self.insts[self.pc].exec);
            exec(self)?;
        }
    }

    pub fn start(&mut self) -> Result<Stop, Fault> {
        self.pc = 0;
        self.execute()
    }

    pub fn get_register(&self, name: &str) -> Result<Value, Fault> {
        self.read(name)
    }

    pub fn set_register(&mut self, name: &str, value: Value) -> Result<(), Fault> {
        self.store(name, value)
    }
}
@end example

@noindent
These methods (and many builders in @ref{5.2.2} and @ref{5.2.3}) resolve
register names against the machine's table once, at assembly time; the check
the book spreads over its @code{get-register} calls is

@example rust
/// Validates a register name against the machine's
/// table, the check the book spreads over its
/// get-register calls at assembly time.
fn check_register(
    registers: &HashSet<String>,
    name: &str,
) -> Result<(), Fault> {
    if registers.contains(name) {
        return Ok(());
    }
    Err(Fault::UnknownRegister {
        reg: name.to_owned(),
    })
}
@end example

