@subsection The Assembler

The assembler transforms the sequence of controller expressions for a machine
into a corresponding list of machine instructions, each with its execution
procedure.  Overall, the assembler is much like the evaluators we studied in
@ref{Chapter 4}---there is an input language (in this case, the
register-machine language) and we must perform an appropriate action for each
type of expression in the language.

The technique of producing an execution procedure for each instruction is just
what we used in @ref{4.1.7} to speed up the evaluator by separating
analysis from runtime execution.  As we saw in @ref{Chapter 4}, much useful
analysis of Scheme expressions could be performed without knowing the actual
values of variables.  Here, analogously, much useful analysis of
register-machine-language expressions can be performed without knowing the
actual contents of machine registers.  For example, we can replace references
to registers by pointers to the register objects, and we can replace references
to labels by pointers to the place in the instruction sequence that the label
designates.

Before it can generate the instruction execution procedures, the assembler must
know what all the labels refer to, so it begins by scanning the controller text
to separate the labels from the instructions.  As it scans the text, it
constructs both a list of instructions and a table that associates each label
with a pointer into that list.  Then the assembler augments the instruction
list by inserting the execution procedure for each instruction.

The @code{install_program} method is the main entry to the assembler.  It
takes the controller datums, scanned from the controller text by the shared
reader of @ref{5.1}, and stores the instruction sequence in the model.
@code{install_program} calls @code{extract_labels} to build the instruction
list and label table from the datums; the scan returns both at once as one
scanned controller, and the installer uses @code{update_insts} to generate the
instruction execution procedures and insert them into the instruction list.

@example rust
impl Machine {
    /// The book's `assemble`, the assembler's main entry:
    /// scans the datums into instructions and labels,
    /// then augments the instructions with their
    /// execution procedures.
    fn install_program(&mut self, datums: &[Value]) -> Result<(), Fault> {
        let scanned = extract_labels(datums)?;
        self.update_insts(&scanned)
    }
}
@end example

@noindent
@code{extract_labels} takes the controller datums and returns two values at
once: (1) the list @code{insts} of instruction data structures, each containing
an instruction from the text; and (2) a table called @code{labels}, which
associates each label with the index in the list @code{insts} that the label
designates.

@example rust
pub fn extract_labels(datums: &[Value]) -> Result<ScannedController, Fault> {
    let mut scanned = ScannedController::default();
    let mut pending: Vec<String> = Vec::new();
    let mut seen: HashSet<String> = HashSet::new();
    for datum in datums {
        let Value::Sym(name) = datum else {
            let index = scanned.insts.len();
            for label in pending.drain(..) {
                scanned.labels.push((label, index));
            }
            let labels: Vec<String> = scanned
                .labels
                .iter()
                .filter(|(_, at)| *at == index)
                .map(|(name, _)| name.clone())
                .collect();
            scanned.insts.push(ScannedInstruction {
                text: render_datum(datum),
                datum: datum.clone(),
                labels,
            });
            continue;
        };
        if !seen.insert(name.to_string()) {
            return Err(Fault::DuplicateLabel {
                label: name.to_string(),
            });
        }
        pending.push(name.to_string());
    }
    // A trailing label names one past the last instruction.
    for label in pending.drain(..) {
        scanned.labels.push((label, scanned.insts.len()));
    }
    Ok(scanned)
}
@end example

@noindent
@code{Extract-labels} works by sequentially scanning the elements of the
@code{text} and accumulating the @code{insts} and the @code{labels}.  If an
element is a symbol (and thus a label) an appropriate entry is added to the
@code{labels} table.  Otherwise the element is accumulated onto the
@code{insts} list.@footnote{The book's Scheme scan smuggles its two values out through a continuation
procedure; the Rust scan returns them directly as one scanned controller, so
this edition's listing above is the value-returning alternative the text
shows.  As it goes, the scan also refuses a repeated label, the defect of
exercise 5.8:

@example rust
/// The scan's two values, returned together: the
/// instructions in order and the label table.
struct ScannedController {
    insts: Vec<ScannedInstruction>,
    labels: Vec<(String, usize)>,
}
@end example

@noindent
and the assembler hands the scanned controller straight to the
execution-procedure builder:

@example rust
// one scan, one pair: install_program is exactly the
// extract_labels call above, then update_insts below
@end example

An argument like the book's @code{receive} that is the next procedure to be
invoked is called a ``continuation.''  Recall that we also used continuations
to implement the backtracking control structure in the @code{amb} evaluator in
@ref{4.3.3}.}

@code{update_insts} modifies the instruction list, which initially contains
only the text of the instructions, to include the corresponding execution
procedures:

@example rust
impl Machine {
    /// The book's `update-insts!`: builds the one
    /// execution procedure each instruction will ever
    /// need, resolving labels, registers, and operations
    /// at assembly time, and records the instruction-use
    /// summary of exercise 5.12.
    fn update_insts(&mut self, scanned: &ScannedController) -> Result<(), Fault> {
        let registers: HashSet<String> =
            self.registers.keys().cloned().collect();
        let labels: HashMap<String, usize> = scanned
            .labels
            .iter()
            .map(|(name, index)| (name.clone(), *index))
            .collect();
        let mut slots = Vec::with_capacity(scanned.insts.len());
        for inst in &scanned.insts {
            let exec = make_execution_procedure(
                &inst.datum,
                &labels,
                &self.ops,
                &registers,
            )?;
            slots.push(Slot {
                text: inst.text.clone(),
                labels: inst.labels.clone(),
                exec,
            });
        }
        self.insts = slots;
        self.labels = labels;
        self.use_summary = summarize(scanned);
        Ok(())
    }
}
@end example

@noindent
The machine instruction data structure simply pairs the instruction text with
the corresponding execution procedure.  The execution procedure is not yet
available when @code{extract-labels} constructs the instruction, and is
inserted later by @code{update-insts!}.

@example rust
/// One assembled instruction: its text, the labels that
/// immediately precede it (retained for exercise 5.17),
/// and the execution procedure: one step over the
/// machine.
struct Slot {
    text: String,
    dominating: Option<(String, usize)>,
    labels: Vec<String>,
    exec: Exec,
}

/// The execution-procedure type: one step over the
/// machine.
type Exec = Rc<dyn Fn(&mut Machine) -> Result<(), Fault>>;
@end example

@noindent
The instruction text is not used by our simulator, but it is handy to keep
around for debugging (see @ref{Exercise 5.16}).

Elements of the label table are pairs:

@example rust
/// The label table's entries: each pairs the label name
/// with the index of the instruction it dominates.
fn make_label_entry(label_name: String, index: usize) -> (String, usize) {
    (label_name, index)
}
@end example

@noindent
Entries will be looked up in the table with

@example rust
/// The book's `lookup-label`: the instruction index a
/// label names, or the typed unknown-label fault.
pub fn lookup_label(
    labels: &[(String, usize)],
    name: &str,
) -> Result<usize, Fault> {
    labels.iter().find(|(seen, _)| seen == name).map_or(
        Err(Fault::UnknownLabel {
            label: name.to_owned(),
        }),
        |(_, index)| Ok(*index),
    )
}
@end example

@quotation
@strong{@anchor{Exercise 5.8}Exercise 5.8:} The following register-machine code
is ambiguous, because the label @code{here} is defined more than once:

@example
start
  (goto (label here))
here
  (assign a (const 3))
  (goto (label there))
here
  (assign a (const 4))
  (goto (label there))
there
@end example

With the simulator as written, what will the contents of register @code{a} be
when control reaches @code{there}?  Modify the @code{extract-labels} procedure
so that the assembler will signal an error if the same label name is used to
indicate two different locations.
@end quotation

